// Test: `==` on a function is IDENTITY — BUG-ZYB-012.
//
// Two names for one function are equal; two functions with the same body are
// not. That is what identity means, and it is what Python, JavaScript and Rust
// answer about a function reference.
//
// Neither half was decided before v0.0.9. Both Rust engines said `#0` to every
// comparison, including a function against itself — a thing that is not equal
// to itself. The browser engine said `#1` to every comparison, including a
// named function against a lambda, because its fallback compared `a.v === b.v`
// and a function has no `v`: two `undefined`s. It looked right on the one case
// anybody had tried and was wrong on all the others.
//
// The gate could not see any of it, and the reason is worth writing down: `zyq
// consensus` compares what programs PRINT, and **no corpus file compared two
// functions**. The divergence was not hidden, it was somewhere nobody had
// looked — which is the other way a green gate means nothing. This file is that
// place now.

uno(x) { <~ x + 1 }
dos(x) { <~ x + 1 }        // el mismo cuerpo, otra función
otra(x) { <~ x * 9 }

// ── the same function under two names ───────────────────────────────────────
a = uno
b = uno
>> "misma, dos nombres    " (a == b) ¶
>> "y contra sí misma     " (uno == uno) ¶

// ── two functions are two functions, however alike ──────────────────────────
>> "mismo cuerpo, distinta" (uno == dos) ¶
>> "distinta de verdad    " (uno == otra) ¶
>> "y `<>` está de acuerdo" (uno <> dos) ¶

// ── a lambda is the evaluation that made it ─────────────────────────────────
l = (x -> x)
m = l                      // el mismo cierre, otro nombre
n = (x -> x)               // otra evaluación, otro cierre
>> "misma lambda          " (l == m) ¶
>> "dos lambdas iguales   " (l == n) ¶

// Written once and evaluated twice is twice: each turn of the loop closes over
// its own `i`, so the two are not one function even though the source is.
hechas = []
i = 1
@ i <= 2 {
    hechas = hechas $+ (x -> x + i)
    i = i + 1
}
>> "dos vueltas del bucle " (hechas[1] == hechas[2]) ¶
>> "y cada una es sí misma" (hechas[1] == hechas[1]) ¶

// ── a named function and a lambda are never the same ────────────────────────
>> "función contra lambda " (uno == l) ¶
>> "función contra entero " (uno == 1) ¶

// ── it survives being passed around, which is the whole use ─────────────────
// A function handed to a higher-order function is still the same function, so
// a caller can check what it was given.
recibe(f) { <~ (f == uno) }
>> "recibida en una HOF   " recibe(uno) ¶
>> "y la otra no lo es    " recibe(dos) ¶

// And the same across a module boundary: two calls to an accessor hand out the
// same definition, which is what GAP-ZYB-005's probes could not assert until
// this was decided.
